---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";
import ConceptMap from "../components/ConceptMap.astro";
import Doodle from "../components/Doodle.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";
---

<Doc
  title="The map"
  current="map"
  description="Explore machine learning methods and see how they relate to one another."
>
  <div class="opener">
    <div>
      <h1>The field map</h1>
      <p class="lede">Explore the main areas of machine learning and see how the ideas connect.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="opener__art"><Doodle name="compass" /></div>
  </div>

  <p>Use the map while you read, or browse it to find a topic. Solid boxes link to lessons in this course. Dashed boxes show related topics that are outside the main path.</p>

  <p>When you meet a new method, ask three questions:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>What feedback does it use?</strong> Examples may have labels, no labels, or rewards that arrive after actions.</li>
    <li><strong>What task does it solve?</strong> It may classify, predict a number, rank items, find groups, or generate new examples.</li>
    <li><strong>What model and training method does it use?</strong> The model represents patterns; the training method adjusts it to fit the data.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A phrase such as “supervised classification with boosted trees” answers all three questions: it describes the feedback, task, and model family.</p>

  <ConceptMap />

  <p>Each box opens a short description when you focus or hover over it. Follow links on solid boxes to the matching lesson. Use the <a href={withBase("/extras/")}>optional guides</a> for more detail on some of the other methods.</p>

  <h2>How to read the map</h2>
  <p>The map is a guide to the subject, not a ranking. A dashed topic can be useful; it is simply outside the scope of this introduction. Return as you learn and connect new terms to the ideas you already know.</p>

  <div class="pip-note">
    <Doodle name="pipThink" decorative />
    <p>
      When you meet a new method, place it on the map. It usually changes the data, the task, the model, or the way the model is trained.
    </p>
  </div>
</Doc>
